---
import Card from "./Card/index.astro";
import Pulse from "./Pulse.astro";
import {NEW_TAB_NOTICE, NOW} from "../lib/constants";
import {iconClass} from "../lib/icons";

/*
 * THIS CARD USED TO HAND-ROLL ITS OWN HEADING, and that was the defect rather than a
 * style choice — reported as the Now card breaking the heading-to-body convention the
 * other five cards keep.
 *
 * It is worth being precise about the cause, because the obvious reading is wrong. The
 * gap under this heading measured 9px against 20px everywhere else, ink to ink at
 * 1440x900, which looks like a spacing value someone got wrong. It was not: `Card` owns
 * that space and gives it to every card that passes a `title`, and this card could not
 * pass one. The "what's
 * that ?" link SHARED THE HEADING'S ROW — heading and link stacked in a flex column,
 * with the live dot pushed to the far end — so the heading had to be written out here,
 * and a heading written out here inherits nothing.
 *
 * So the fix is structural and the spacing follows from it. The link becomes an icon in
 * the card's own corner slot beside the dot, the heading goes back to being
 * `title="Now"`, and the 20px arrives on its own. Nothing in this file sets a spacing
 * value any more.
 *
 * WHY THE CORNER AND NOT INSIDE THE HEADING. An adornment after the title text was the
 * first shape tried, and it is worse for the reason that matters most about a heading:
 * a link inside the heading element becomes part of that heading's accessible name, so
 * screen-reader heading navigation would announce "Now What's a /now page?" instead of
 * "Now". The
 * corner keeps the heading's name exactly one word, and still reads as belonging to the
 * card rather than to any line of its copy.
 *
 * The icon is 1em inside a 1.5rem anchor — 24px at the default text size, which is SC
 * 2.5.8's minimum target on the nose, and it grows with the reader's text rather than
 * staying put. It is a FLEX BOX so that it centres the glyph, which is what nine other
 * icons on this page already rely on: a flex item is blockified, so the preset's
 * baseline nudge does not apply to it and does not need to.
 *
 * The token says `inline-flex`, and what the browser computes here is `flex` — the marks
 * group around it is itself a flex container, so this anchor is a flex item and is
 * blockified like its neighbours. An earlier version of this note justified the token by
 * saying a padded INLINE anchor would report a 24px target while overlapping the line
 * above it. That is true of an inline box in general and cannot happen where this anchor
 * sits, so it was the wrong reason for the right token; the token is kept because it is
 * what the box means when read on its own.
 */
---

<Card colSpan="md:col-span-1" rowSpan="md:row-span-2" title="Now">
    <Fragment slot="corner">
        <a
                href={NOW.explainer_url}
                target="_blank"
                class="inline-flex items-center justify-center w-6 h-6 text-base text-[var(--text)] hover:text-[var(--accent)] transition-colors duration-300 ease-in-out"
        >
            <span class={`${iconClass(NOW.explainer_icon)} shrink-0`} aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="sr-only">{NOW.explainer_name}</span>
            {/* A second sr-only span rather than a longer explainer_name: that string is
                the SUBJECT half of the name — what the destination explains — and this one
                says what the link DOES, which is only useful once the reader knows what the
                link is. Announced together: "What's a /now page? (opens in a new tab)". */}
            <span class="sr-only">{NEW_TAB_NOTICE}</span>
        </a>
        <Pulse/>
    </Fragment>
    <p class="text-sm">{NOW.description}</p>
</Card>
